script:
export const meta = {
  name: 'hail-b1-build',
  description: 'Build B1 of the hail history (store as site files, click-anywhere sheet, town tables) with three builders, four reviewers, skeptic verification, fixers and a re-check',
  phases: [
    { title: 'Skeleton', detail: 'website/hail.py public API, words, fixture store, grid tests' },
    { title: 'Build', detail: 'three builders: store, site, docs' },
    { title: 'Review', detail: 'four reviewers: store equality, browser, rules, words' },
    { title: 'Verify', detail: 'one skeptic per finding' },
    { title: 'Fix', detail: 'fixers by file ownership' },
    { title: 'Recheck', detail: 'tests, builds, smoke, measurements' },
  ],
}

const S = '/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-RoofingApp/7638440a-a008-5ef8-a289-86be0a23e7a7/scratchpad'
const WT = `${S}/wt-hail-b1`
const BRIEF = `${S}/hail-build-brief.md`
const PROTO = `${S}/wt-hail/research/hail-history/prototype`
const PROTO_STORE = `${S}/hail-store/h1-mesh`
const DL = `${S}/hail-dl/mesh-backfill`
const VENV = `${S}/hail-venv/bin/python`
const OUT = `${S}/hail-b1-out`
const OWNER = `The owner (Carson) wrote on 2026-10-18, verbatim: "Decisions that are mine. 1. Trailing 5 years to today. 2. A public lookup by typed address or clicked location. Each address in montana should have a dot to click on the map with a popup on each history of weather. 3. Pages only for now. 4. Make the weather updates consistent and recent as possible 5. Try to find and read hail units and codes then process it and make the user understand it easier. Where they can find the exact date that the hail happened at that location. 6. Don't include owners names. But I do want all addresses included and all types of weather at that address for the past 5 years in a popup after you click the house on the map. 7. Follow the laws"`

const COMMON = `You work in the git worktree ${WT} (branch claude/hail-b1, based on commit 825d6c7, which includes the P2 satellite layer and the imagery base map). Read ${BRIEF} in full first: the section "B1 interface spec" and "Additions fixed on 2026-10-18" are the contract every builder follows; do not change the contract. Then read the repository rules that apply to your files (AGENTS.md is already in your context): Python first (rule 4), evidence honesty (rule 5), feed contracts (rule 7), real-browser checks (rule 8), no "best" claims (rule 9), plain wording in sentence case, no em-dashes, no model, AI-vendor or AI-product names anywhere in repository files, tests with every behaviour change. Python 3.12 is CI's version; the site build must need no pip package beyond requests, jinja2, pillow, numpy, pygrib, h5py, tzdata (pyarrow is allowed only in the compare subcommand, imported lazily). Edit only the files you own (listed below); other builders are editing the other files at the same time in the same worktree, so never run git checkout, stash, reset, add, commit or push, and never reformat or rewrite a file you do not own. Scratch output goes under ${OUT}/<your-name>/ (create it), never under the worktree. When you finish, return a plain report: what you built, the commands you ran with their results and measured numbers, and anything you could not do.`

const SKELETON = `${COMMON}

You are the skeleton builder. You own website/hail.py, tests/test_hail.py and website/data/hail-fixture/. Write website/hail.py with the complete public API named in the brief (constants, CLASSES, WORDS, HailStoreError, cell_for, cell_centre, shard_path, cell_key, day_index, day_of_index, inches, class_for, load_index, cell_events, describe, map_data, fixture_store, and the argparse CLI with subcommands build, verify, compare, fixture, fetch). Implement fully now: the grid arithmetic (match the prototype at ${PROTO}/mesh_history.py lines 60 to 140 exactly; cell indexes must round-trip at tile edges), the words and classes (the brief's text; every sentence a template with braces; the classes with min and max in tenths and in inches, the NWS size words, the severe flag, the 4.00 in note), inches() (tenths to a one-decimal string: 513 to "2.0", 254 to "1.0", 64 to "0.3"), describe() (fills the templates in Python for a list of (date, tenths) events plus the index, newest first, with the lead, rows, what-this-is, missing, stale and source lines, and SAMPLE when sample=True), map_data() (the brief's map.json["hail"] block with tenths_per_inch 254), the shard and index and manifest readers, cell_events() from a store directory, and fixture_store(dest) which writes a small SAMPLE store (format hail-store-v1: hail/index.json, hail/manifest.json and two shards: the tile holding Benchland 47.08 N 110.02 W with the 2025-06-15 SPC day at 513 tenths in Benchland's cell plus a few neighbouring cells and days you invent and label as sample in index.json["sample"]=true, and the tile holding Billings 45.783 N 108.5 W with three days) and commit nothing. Run fixture_store into website/data/hail-fixture/ (it must stay under 50 KB) so the site builder can use it. Leave build, verify, compare and fetch_store as functions that raise NotImplementedError with a one-line message (the store builder fills them next), but define their signatures and docstrings now. Write tests/test_hail.py for everything you implemented: grid round trips at the four corners of Montana and at tile edges, shard_path and cell_key examples, day_index for 2025-06-15 (2022 is 2025-06-15 minus 2020-01-01 in days; compute it), inches rounding, class boundaries at 64, 190, 191, 254, 381, 508, 698, 699 tenths and the 4.00 in note at 1016, describe() output for the Benchland fixture (contains "2.0 in", "51.3 mm", "Jun 15, 2025", the severe tag, no forbidden term), the forbidden-term check over every WORDS value (damage, damaged, dry, clear, insurance, eligible, confirmed; "no hail" only inside the one negation sentence), map_data keys, the fixture's size and manifest hashes. Run python -m unittest tests.test_hail and make it pass. Report the API as written (signatures) so the other builders can rely on it.`

const STORE = `${COMMON}

You are the store builder. You own website/hail.py (the skeleton builder has already written its public API, words, fixture and readers; keep every public name and behaviour, and fill in the rest), tests/test_hail.py (add tests; keep the existing ones passing), .github/workflows/hail-store.yml (new) and website/data/hail-fixture/ (regenerate only if the format changes). Implement in website/hail.py: (1) fetch_store(get, dest_dir, url) which downloads the release asset through the given get callable (requests-like; follows redirects), checks it is a gzip tar, unpacks only the hail/ tree into dest_dir, verifies every sha256 in hail/manifest.json and the counts in hail/index.json, and raises HailStoreError with plain words on any failure; plus load_store(path) which accepts a directory or a tar.gz (for --hail-store); (2) the build subcommand: the trailing window of 1,826 SPC days ending at the newest 12:00 UTC MESH_Max_1440min_00.50 file published in the NOAA bucket at build time (list the bucket for today's and yesterday's stamps to find it), downloading each stamp's file into --cache (reuse a cached file when present; the cache at ${DL} already holds 1,825 stamps 2021-10-17 to 2026-10-16), decoding with pygrib (lazy import) exactly as the prototype does (port pack_tenths and the state mask from ${PROTO}/mesh_history.py, using the Montana polygon in website/static/map/basemap.json; the prototype's grid.json and mask.bin at ${PROTO_STORE} are the reference), with multiprocessing (--procs), the copy rule from the brief (HEAD both copies; on a byte-size difference re-derive the day from the 720 two-minute MESH_00.50 frames of that SPC day and record it; when IEM does not answer record skipped), writing the shards, index.json and manifest.json in the brief's format, and a ledger of what was fetched (size, ETag, last-modified) under the store; a missing stamp is recorded in missing_days and never written as zero; (3) verify: re-read the store, recompute manifest hashes, check counts, check the Benchland cell for SPC day 2025-06-15 holds 513 tenths; (4) compare --store DIR --prototype ${PROTO_STORE}: map the prototype's crop cell indexes to global row and col (its grid.json crop and mask.bin), read its events.parquet with pyarrow under ${VENV} (run that part as a subprocess with ${VENV} if the system python lacks pyarrow), and report mismatches on the overlapping days (the gate is 0); (5) fetch: download the asset to a path. Then write .github/workflows/hail-store.yml exactly as the brief describes (daily 12:43 UTC and workflow_dispatch, contents: write, Python 3.12, pip install numpy pygrib requests, build with an Actions cache of the downloaded files keyed by month that is optional, verify, tar, gh release create if missing then gh release upload --clobber; timeout 60 minutes; concurrency group hail-store). Then RUN the real build here: python -m website.hail build --out ${OUT}/store/store --cache ${DL} --procs 6 (network is available for the newest stamps and the copy-rule HEADs), then verify, then compare. Measure and report: days present and missing, events, shards, bytes of hail/ on disk, the largest shard in bytes, build wall time, the copy-check results (which days differed, which were re-derived, whether IEM answered), and the compare result. Then tar it as ${OUT}/store/hail-store.tar.gz (tar czf, -C store hail) and report its bytes; the site builder and reviewers use that tar through --hail-store. Add tests for fetch_store (a fake get serving a tar built from the fixture; a bad hash; a non-tar body), load_store (dir and tar), the copy-rule decision function (pure function on two sizes and an IEM-missing case), pack_tenths (port the prototype's test cases: -1 and -3 never stored, 6.35 mm at the floor, rounding), the stamp and SPC day rule, missing-day handling, index and manifest writing. Keep python -m unittest tests.test_hail passing.`

const SITE = `${COMMON}

You are the site builder. You own website/build.py, website/sources.py, website/templates/map.html, website/templates/town.html, website/templates/about.html, website/templates/base.html (only if needed), website/static/map/map.js, website/static/map/map.css, tools/smoke_map.py, tests/test_site.py (additions only) and .github/workflows/website.yml. The store builder is filling website/hail.py at the same time; its public API (names and behaviour) is already in the file: read it, import it, never edit it; if you need something it lacks, write a small helper in build.py and say so in your report. Build: (1) website/build.py: flag --hail-history (default off) and option --hail-store PATH; with the flag, Collector.hail loads the store (hail.fetch_store through the collector's get from hail.STORE_URL, or hail.load_store(PATH)), copies the hail/ tree into the output, adds data/map.json["hail"] from hail.map_data (state loaded, unavailable or sample; the problem words on failure; with --sample use website/data/hail-fixture/ and sample=True), registers a "hail_history" status like the other sources so --require-live fails on a failed load, and passes each town's history (hail.cell_events plus hail.describe) to the town template; with the flag off, no hail key, no files, no section: the flag-off build must be byte-identical to the base commit's build except the shared map script's ?v= hash (a reviewer will diff two builds). (2) website/sources.py: CONTRACTS["hail_history"] per the brief, gated like the storm contracts so the About page shows it only with the flag (follow how STORM_CONTRACTS is handled in build.py). (3) Templates: map.html gets the hail sheet (reuse the existing sheet pattern and classes from the town card sheet; a heading, a lead paragraph, a list for the day rows, the what-this-is, missing, stale and source paragraphs; a close button; aria attributes like the existing sheet), the legend line, and the noscript and List template words, all gated on the flag through the template context; town.html gets the "Hail history, five years" section rendered from the describe() dict (table of date, size in inches and mm, class word, severe tag; the lead; what-this-is; missing; stale; source; SAMPLE label), gated on the flag; about.html gets a short "Hail history" paragraph (what the store is, the radar-estimate kind, the floor, the day rule, the daily job, the source and the units table, "an estimate is not an inspection"), gated on the flag. (4) map.js: a documented section (header bullet plus a function block like setBase): on a click or tap on the map that is not on a bubble, control, popover or sheet, compute row and col from map.json.hail.grid, test the Montana mask by asking the shard (a 404 or an empty cell list means no events: outside the state is decided by the basemap's Montana polygon the script already loads for the outline, so use that point-in-polygon only if the code already has one; otherwise treat "no shard" as no events and let the words cover it, but then say so in your report), fetch the shard (relative URL from map.json.hail.shard with {r} and {c} filled with four-digit zero-padded numbers), build the rows from the cell's [dayIndex, tenths] pairs (date from d0 plus dayIndex via UTC date arithmetic formatted as "Jun 15, 2025"; inches from tenths divided by map.json.hail.tenths_per_inch rounded to a tenth; the class from the classes list by comparing tenths with min_tenths and max_tenths), fill the Python templates from map.json.hail.words by replacing the brace placeholders, open the sheet, write ?at=lat,lon into the URL (saveUrl) and open from ?at= on arrival (pan there at zoom 9 or the current zoom if higher); the unavailable words on fetch failure; the loading words while fetching; Escape and the close button close it. No threshold, unit, class word or sentence literal in map.js (the strings "25.4", "254", "6.35", every class word and every template sentence are forbidden; a test greps for them). map.css: the sheet's hail list styles using the existing tokens. (5) tools/smoke_map.py: with a build that has data/map.json["hail"], add checks: a click at Benchland (47.08, -110.02) opens the sheet with "2.0 in", "51.3 mm" and "Jun 15, 2025"; a click far outside Montana shows the outside or no-events words and makes no shard request outside the site; the request log holds only site files; a build without the hail key has no sheet and the click does nothing; screenshots at 390 and 1280 px of the open sheet when --shots is given. (6) tests/test_site.py: flag off produces no hail key, files or section; --sample with the flag produces the SAMPLE words on the sheet, the town section and the About paragraph; map.js forbidden strings; the template placeholders in words match the keys map.js fills; the contract row appears only with the flag; a failed store load marks the source failed and keeps the pages building without --require-live. (7) .github/workflows/website.yml: branch previews pass --hail-history next to --storm-layers; main does not; the test step runs tests.test_hail. Run: python -m unittest discover -s tests; python -m website.build --out ${OUT}/site/off --sample --now 2026-10-18T12:00:00Z; the same with --hail-history into ${OUT}/site/sample; and when ${OUT}/store/hail-store.tar.gz exists (the store builder writes it; poll for it every 2 minutes for up to 40 minutes while you do other work), python -m website.build --out ${OUT}/site/real --sample --hail-history --hail-store ${OUT}/store/hail-store.tar.gz; then PW_CHROMIUM=/opt/pw-browsers/chromium python tools/smoke_map.py on each build (read the script's usage first), with --shots on the real one. Report the commands, results, bytes of _site with and without the flag, and anything not done.`

const DOCS = `${COMMON}

You are the docs builder. You own docs/DECISIONS.md, docs/DESIGN.md, docs/ROADMAP.md, README.md, AGENTS.md and the new file research/hail-history/validation-evidence/b1-verification.json (create the folder; the research README itself lives on another branch, do not create it here). ${OWNER} Write: (1) docs/DECISIONS.md: a new entry D34 after D32 (the file on this branch ends at D32; D33 is the schedule offset on another branch, so write "D33 is on the schedule-offset branch" in one parenthesis) titled "Hail history for every address: the owner's decisions and the B1 build (2026-10-18)". It quotes the owner's seven sentences verbatim as a numbered list, then records what they settle: the trailing window of 1,826 SPC days to the newest published day; a public lookup by clicked location now and by address and address dots later (B3), with no owner names; GitHub Pages only, so the store is site files published by a daily job as one release asset the 10-minute build downloads; updates within one build of the 12:00 UTC file (job 12:43 UTC) with the schedule offset as the consistency fix; the units and codes verified from NOAA's MRMS GRIB2 user table (mm; -1 Missing; -3 No Coverage; reflectivity -99 and -999; versions 12.0 to 12.2 agree; the archive files do not state their version) so sizes show in inches to a tenth with mm kept and the NWS size words, and the exact SPC day of each estimate; the 0.25 in floor (pea size, the smallest NWS report size) and why; the 4.00 in review rule; the kinds kept apart and the sentences that say an estimate is not an inspection and that no estimate is not "no hail"; the flag --hail-history off on main until the owner says the About text is right and the counsel items (the MSL "for no other purpose" clause, MCA 2-6-1017, GitHub Actions and Pages terms) are confirmed, per "Follow the laws"; and *Why:* in the house style. Match the existing entries' voice; read D30, D31 (on another branch; skip) and D32 first. (2) docs/DESIGN.md: the hail sheet and town table states (loaded, no events, outside, loading, unavailable, stale, sample), their words, the legend line, the size words table, and the data contract (map.json["hail"], the shard format, the index), in the file's existing structure. (3) docs/ROADMAP.md: a Done row for B1 under its flag and Next items for B2 (joins), B3 (addresses and search; the legal go-live gate), B4 (live swaths), each one line, plus the measurement line for the daily job. (4) README.md: the hail history in the feature list and the build flag and the hail-store workflow in the commands or layout, matching the file's style. (5) AGENTS.md: the layout block gains hail.py and the hail-store workflow in one line each; the Commands block gains the flag; nothing else changes. (6) b1-verification.json: a skeleton with "checked_at_utc", "branch", "base_commit", "gates" (each gate from the brief's B1 list with "status": "pending" and an empty "evidence"), "measurements" (empty object) and "notes"; the reviewers fill it. Em-dashes 0 in your added lines; no model, AI-vendor or AI-product names; every number you write must come from the brief, the owner's text or a file you cite (do not invent build measurements; write "measured by the reviewers" where a number is pending). Report the diff stat of your files.`

const FINDINGS = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    findings: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: {
          id: { type: 'string' }, file: { type: 'string' }, line: { type: 'integer' },
          claim: { type: 'string', description: 'the defect in one sentence' },
          evidence: { type: 'string', description: 'the command output or quoted text that shows it' },
          fix: { type: 'string', description: 'the exact change to make, with the quoted old text where possible' },
          severity: { type: 'string', enum: ['blocking', 'should', 'minor'] },
          confidence: { type: 'number' },
        },
        required: ['id', 'file', 'line', 'claim', 'evidence', 'fix', 'severity', 'confidence'],
      },
    },
    measurements: { type: 'object', description: 'every number you measured, by name', additionalProperties: true },
    gates: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'object', properties: { gate: { type: 'string' }, status: { type: 'string', enum: ['pass', 'fail', 'not_checked'] }, evidence: { type: 'string' } }, required: ['gate', 'status', 'evidence'] } },
    summary: { type: 'string' },
  },
  required: ['findings', 'measurements', 'gates', 'summary'],
}

const VERDICT = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    id: { type: 'string' }, refuted: { type: 'boolean' }, reason: { type: 'string' },
    fix_ok: { type: 'boolean' }, adjusted_fix: { type: 'string' },
  },
  required: ['id', 'refuted', 'reason', 'fix_ok', 'adjusted_fix'],
}

const REVIEW_COMMON = `Read-only reviewer in ${WT} (branch claude/hail-b1; base commit 825d6c7). Read ${BRIEF} first (the B1 interface spec, the gates, the additions). Never edit, stage, commit or push. Scratch under ${OUT}/<your-name>/. Report concrete defects only, each with the file, line, the evidence (command output or quoted text) and an exact fix; rank blocking first. Fill the gates list for the gates you checked and put every number you measured in measurements.`

const REVIEWERS = [
  { key: 'store', prompt: `${REVIEW_COMMON} Lens: the store. (1) Read website/hail.py and tests/test_hail.py in full against the spec: grid arithmetic identical to ${PROTO}/mesh_history.py (write and run a script that checks cell_for and shard_path on 10,000 random Montana points against the prototype's formulas), pack_tenths behaviour (-1 and -3 never stored; floor 64; rounding), the SPC day rule, the window definition, missing-day handling, the manifest and index formats exactly as the brief writes them, fetch_store's checks (bad hash, non-tar, path traversal in tar members, members outside hail/). (2) Open the built store at ${OUT}/store/store (and the tar ${OUT}/store/hail-store.tar.gz): count shards, events and bytes yourself; check 20 random shards parse and keep sorted, integer-only cell lists; run python -m website.hail verify and compare yourself and quote the output; independently re-decode three stamps from ${DL} with pygrib and the prototype's pack_tenths and compare their Montana cells with the store's shards (0 mismatches expected); check index.json's as_of, window, days_expected and missing_days against the bucket listing (curl the bucket prefix for the newest stamps) and against the files in the cache. (3) Review .github/workflows/hail-store.yml: parse it with Python yaml; check the cron, permissions, the gh release commands, the timeout, the concurrency group, the Python version, the pip line; simulate the job's steps locally in order (build from the cache is already done; run verify and tar again) and note anything the runner would do differently (no cache, 2 CPUs, fresh network). Gates: store equals the prototype on every overlapping cell-day; hail/ at most 60 MB on disk; verify passes; the Benchland cell holds 513 tenths on 2025-06-15.` },
  { key: 'browser', prompt: `${REVIEW_COMMON} Lens: the site in a real browser (rule 8). Builds: python -m website.build --out ${OUT}/browser/off --sample --now 2026-10-18T12:00:00Z (flag off), --hail-history --sample into ${OUT}/browser/sample, and --hail-history --sample --hail-store ${OUT}/store/hail-store.tar.gz into ${OUT}/browser/real (each must exit 0; quote failures). Also build the BASE commit flag-off for the byte-identical gate: git -C ${WT} worktree is shared, so instead run git -C ${WT} stash is forbidden; use git -C ${WT} archive 825d6c7 | tar -x -C ${OUT}/browser/base-src and build from there with the same --sample --now into ${OUT}/browser/base-off, then diff -r the two flag-off outputs: the only differences allowed are the shared map script's content and its ?v= hash in pages; list every other difference as a blocking finding. Then with Playwright (PW_CHROMIUM=/opt/pw-browsers/chromium; python tools/smoke_map.py has the harness, read it) on the real build served with python -m http.server: run tools/smoke_map.py and quote its result; then your own script: click at Benchland (47.08, -110.02) and assert the sheet text contains "2.0 in", "51.3 mm", "Jun 15, 2025" and the severe tag; click at Billings and at a point in Wyoming (43.5, -108.0) and at a point in the Pacific (40, -130); record every network request's host (only the local server is allowed; one off-site request is a blocking finding); check ?at=47.08,-110.02 opens the sheet on arrival; check Escape closes it; check the sheet at 390x844 and 1280x800 in light and dark (screenshots into ${OUT}/browser/shots, open them and describe what you see: text legible, no overflow, the sheet does not cover the whole map on desktop); check the town page for Benchland's nearest town or for Billings shows the "Hail history, five years" table with the right rows and the SAMPLE label absent on the real build and present on the sample build; check the flag-off build has no sheet markup, no hail key and no hail/ folder and that a click does nothing; run tools/smoke_pages.py on the real build. Read map.js's new section for rule 4: list every literal it contains that is a threshold, unit, class word or sentence (expected none) and whether the header documents the duty.` },
  { key: 'rules', prompt: `${REVIEW_COMMON} Lens: the repository's rules over the whole diff (git -C ${WT} diff 825d6c7 --stat and the full diff, plus untracked files via git -C ${WT} status --short). Check: Python first (every word the viewer reads is written in Python or a template; map.js only switches, fetches, formats dates and fills templates); evidence honesty (the words never state damage, "no hail" as a fact, an all-clear, insurance or eligibility; missing is said as missing; the radar estimate kind is named; SAMPLE labels on sample output); the contract row (units, timing, cadence, missing rules, copy rule, the units table) and the parser and tests changed together; tests exist for every behaviour change and python -m unittest discover -s tests passes (run it, quote the tail); website.yml passes the flag on branch previews only and runs tests.test_hail; the daily workflow is sound; docs updated (DECISIONS D34 quotes the owner's seven sentences verbatim: compare character by character with this text: ${OWNER}); README, AGENTS, DESIGN and ROADMAP changes match what was built (no claim of a measurement nobody made); em-dashes in added lines (must be 0; count with git diff | grep '^+' | grep -c the em-dash character); the words Claude, Anthropic, Codex, ChatGPT, OpenAI, Copilot, Gemini, the worker model, the larger model, the mid model in added lines (must be 0); plain wording and sentence case in the viewer-facing text; no new pip package for the site build; no hex colours in components (tokens only); the fixture under 50 KB and labelled sample; python desktop/launcher.py --selftest payload still passes (run it).` },
  { key: 'words', prompt: `${REVIEW_COMMON} Lens: the words and the sizes. (1) Verify the NWS hail size chart live: fetch https://www.weather.gov/ and its hail size page (search for the "hail size" chart under weather.gov, for example the Louisville or the SPC "Hail Size Description Chart" pages; if no page is reachable, say so and rate the class words unverified). Compare every class word in website/hail.py CLASSES with the chart's objects and inch values (pea 1/4, marble or mothball 1/2, penny 3/4, nickel 7/8, quarter 1, half dollar 1 1/4, ping-pong ball 1 1/2, golf ball 1 3/4, hen egg 2, tennis ball 2 1/2, baseball 2 3/4, tea cup 3, softball 4, grapefruit 4 1/2); a class word whose range does not match the chart is a finding. (2) Check the inch arithmetic: 513 tenths is 51.3 mm is 2.02 in shown as "2.0 in"; 254 is "1.0"; 64 is "0.3"; the severe threshold is 254 tenths (1.00 in); the floor 64 (0.25 in is 6.35 mm, the smallest stored value 6.4 mm): confirm the code's constants and rounding, and the class bounds in tenths against inches times 25.4. (3) Read every WORDS value and every template string in map.html, town.html, about.html, DESIGN.md's words and the contract row for: forbidden terms (damage, damaged, dry, clear, insurance, eligible, confirmed; "no hail" outside the one negation sentence), a damage probability or any statement of fact about hail at the ground, "best" claims, em-dashes, sentence case, plain words (a roofer and a homeowner must understand each line), the SPC day explanation (12:00 UTC to 12:00 UTC, about 6 am to 6 am Mountain time), the cell size (0.01 degree is about 0.69 miles north-south and about 0.47 miles east-west at 47 N: check the numbers the words use), the "estimate is not an inspection" line, the source line with the as-of date and the next-day expectation, the stale and unavailable lines, the SAMPLE label. (4) Run describe() on the Benchland fixture and on an empty event list and on a stale index and quote the output; check that the placeholders in WORDS match exactly the keys map.js fills (read map.js's fill function) and that no placeholder stays unfilled in the rendered sample build (grep the built pages for "{" inside the hail section).` },
]

const OWNERSHIP = {
  store: ['website/hail.py', 'tests/test_hail.py', '.github/workflows/hail-store.yml', 'website/data/hail-fixture/'],
  site: ['website/build.py', 'website/sources.py', 'website/templates/', 'website/static/map/map.js', 'website/static/map/map.css', 'tools/smoke_map.py', 'tests/test_site.py', '.github/workflows/website.yml'],
  docs: ['docs/', 'README.md', 'AGENTS.md', 'research/hail-history/validation-evidence/b1-verification.json'],
}
function ownerOf(file) {
  for (const [k, prefixes] of Object.entries(OWNERSHIP)) if (prefixes.some((p) => file.startsWith(p) || file.includes(p))) return k
  return 'site'
}

